# Infrastructure Economist Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards at MWAA

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author lens:** Infrastructure economist. Twenty years auditing airport capital programs.
**Assignment:** Cost the decision to specify (or not specify) acoustic, PA, FIDS, and communications requirements before the physical program at IAD and DCA hardens.
**Independence:** No other Stage 1 briefs read.

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **The capital envelope this decision governs is not marginal.** MWAA's adopted 2025 program is roughly $9.0B combined ($6.99B IAD + $2.39B DCA); the July 2026 expansion announcement raised the Dulles program alone to a projected **$20–22.5B over ~10 years** and MWAA has told the bond market it plans to issue **~$5.5B of new debt between 2025 and 2028** on top of the ~$4.9B outstanding. A design standard that touches every substantially renovated square foot in that pipeline is not a stylistic choice; it is a spec that will be priced into contracts totaling nine to eleven figures. [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Infrastructure-Needs-Study.pdf] [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]

2. **US airport infrastructure need has decoupled from the funding mechanisms designed to pay for it.** ACI-NA's 2025 study puts five-year U.S. airport capital needs at **$173.9B (2025–2029), up 15.1% from $151B two years earlier**. The FAA's NPIAS (2023–2027) identifies **$62.4B of AIP-eligible development, up ~43%** from the prior cycle. Meanwhile the Passenger Facility Charge cap has been frozen at **$4.50 per segment since 2000**. The delta gets closed with revenue debt — which is exactly what MWAA is doing. A specification decision that avoids expensive retrofits is a leverage point in the funding stack, not a design refinement. [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Infrastructure-Needs-Study.pdf] [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/npias-2023-2027-narrative.pdf] [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passenger_facility_charge]

3. **The empirical base rate for on-time, on-budget airport megaprojects is low; the base rate for programs that got the design brief wrong at the start is disastrous.** Berlin Brandenburg (BER) opened **nine years late** and ran an initial ~€2.83B budget to roughly **€6.5–7.3B** — a 2.3–2.6× overrun driven substantially by an idiosyncratic downward smoke-extraction system that never certified, escalators that did not reach the correct floors, and 90,000 mislabeled doors. Denver's automated baggage system started as a $250M scope for one concourse for one carrier, then expanded mid-flight to three concourses and twenty carriers; the airport opened **16 months late** with ~$560M of overrun tied to the system. LAX's Automated People Mover was $1.9B in 2018 dollars; a July 2024 settlement pushed the total to **~$3.34B, roughly $1.49B per mile**, and the opening slid from 2023 to 2026, missing the FIFA World Cup deadline the program was justified against. [Source: https://boardinggroup.one/travel/news/berlin-brandenburg-airport-germanys-e7-billion-monument-to-chaos/] [Source: https://www.wrike.com/blog/lessons-learned-from-project-failure-at-denver-international-airport-why-checking-bags-is-still-a-pain/] [Source: https://laist.com/news/transportation/lax-people-mover-delay-grand-jury-report]

4. **The counter-example — Heathrow T5 — validates the "spec early, hold the risk centrally" thesis.** Budgeted £4.3B, delivered at **£4.2B and on time in March 2008**. BAA (the operator) explicitly held all program risk under the T5 Agreement rather than pushing it into fixed-price supplier contracts, and required an integrated design brief before ground broke. That is the closest available analog to what MWAA is being asked to do: define the performance envelope centrally, before individual concourse packages are procured. [Source: https://infrastructuredeliverymodels.gihub.org/case-studies/heathrow-terminal-5/]

5. **The MacLeamy curve is not a slogan; it is the reason this decision has to be made now, not later.** The design/construction literature consistently shows change cost rises **roughly 20× from schematic design to change-order-during-construction**. Acoustic performance, PA zoning, FIDS density, and EVACS intelligibility are all cross-discipline requirements that must be set in schematic design because they drive room geometry, structural attachments, ceiling voids, cable pathways, and RCP coordination. Specifying them after Concourse E opens (autumn 2026) or after the C/D replacement design hardens is exponentially more expensive than specifying them now. [Source: https://www.danieldavis.com/macleamy/]

6. **IAD's cost per enplanement is trending materially higher independent of any quiet-by-design decision.** MWAA's own Official Statement for the June 2025 $714.4M revenue bond issuance forecasts **IAD CPE rising from ~$12.88 (2024) to ~$20.71 by 2030** — a ~61% increase in six years, before the $20–22.5B expansion is priced in. Any additional standard must be defended against that trajectory: the airlines are already writing checks for a rising unit cost. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airports/iad] [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]

7. **The evidence base on acoustic retrofits in existing terminals argues strongly for upfront specification.** ACRP Report 175 (the primary US authority on airport PA intelligibility) documents a representative terminal remediation of **3,200 m² of suspended acoustic baffles plus 800 m² of wall panels at $2.8M** — added to a completed building. That is roughly $700/m² of treated area for a targeted acoustic-only intervention. In a 5-million-square-foot IAD expansion (~465,000 m²), treating even 20% of the envelope post-hoc is a $65M+ line item that a schematic-design specification could avoid at close to zero marginal cost. [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrp0715/wp-content/themes/acrp-child/documents/201/original/acrp_r175.pdf]

8. **MWAA's balance sheet can absorb the standard; the question is not affordability but opportunity cost.** MWAA reported airport-system DSCR of **2.77× in 2023, forecast 2.54× in 2024**, well above the 1.40× U&L target. The ratings (Moody's Aa3 / S&P AA- / Fitch AA-) are stable. So the constraint is not "can we pay for the standard." It is: every dollar of design and commissioning capacity spent unnecessarily late is a dollar not available for the AeroTrain extension ($3.75B budgeted), the Concourse C/D replacement, or the eventual DCA Terminal 1 rebuild. [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/editorial-section/debt-management] [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/05/12/report-new-plan-to-overhaul-dulles-airport-would-eliminate-mobile-lounges/]

