## 2. Evidence section

### 2.1 The macro envelope: what MWAA is committing to

The 2025 U&L took effect January 1, 2025 and runs through 2039. It was signed alongside a $9.0B capital plan (~$7.0B IAD + ~$2.39B DCA). The July 2026 White House/DOT/United/MWAA announcement of a **$20–22.5B expanded Dulles program** replaced the IAD envelope; MWAA has since begun disclosing that the funding structure is unresolved and that CPE trajectories in the current Official Statement do not yet reflect the expanded program. Trade press (Bond Buyer, FFXnow, Cranky Flier) has been openly skeptical of the executability of the announced scope in the announced timeframe. [Source: https://www.airwaysmag.com/new-post/dulles-20-billion-overhaul-mobile-lounges] [Source: https://crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]

For the infrastructure economist, three implications follow. First, the near-term construction pipeline that any MWAA standard will govern includes: Concourse E (~435,000 sq ft, opens autumn 2026 — already too late to influence beyond commissioning); a DCA Terminal 2 refresh (~$63.9M, near-term); the AeroTrain extension (~$3.75B); and the Concourse C/D replacement. Second, the specification decision is bimodal: it either enters the design manual before the C/D replacement RFP goes out, or it enters as a modification later at MacLeamy-curve prices. Third, because the funding structure of the expansion is unresolved, any standard that materially moves CPE is politically constrained by airline signatory concurrence under the U&L.

### 2.2 The base rate on terminal megaprojects

The historical distribution of large terminal projects is not normal; it has a long tail. BER is the modal cautionary tale — €2.83B budget, ~€6.5–7.3B delivered, nine-year delay — and the specific mechanism of failure was a bespoke, insufficiently specified emergency system that could not be certified after construction. The system tried to push smoke down through the floor instead of up through the roof; it was unprecedented and it did not work. Post-hoc redesign of a life-safety system in a completed building is the paradigmatic example of the MacLeamy curve realized. [Source: https://boardinggroup.one/travel/news/berlin-brandenburg-airport-germanys-e7-billion-monument-to-chaos/]

Denver's baggage system is the direct cross-discipline analog to acoustic and PA specification. It failed because scope grew mid-project (from one concourse and one carrier to three and twenty) without re-baselining, and because the contractor accepted a two-year delivery on a four-year design. GAO's contemporaneous review documents ~$560M of overrun and 16 months of delay. LAX APM tells the same story in current dollars: the settlement package pushed the project to $3.34B and slid the opening past its justifying event. [Source: https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-95-35br.pdf] [Source: https://www.westsidecurrent.com/la_city_news/lax-built-one-of-the-priciest-airport-trains-in-the-u-s-years-later-it/article_a54d1b77-095e-4c23-b50e-ee6d09dac268.html]

Heathrow T5 is the positive control. £4.3B budgeted, £4.2B delivered, on-time March 2008, in a UK infrastructure environment where every studied comparable had opened late. The mechanism was procurement discipline: BAA held program risk centrally rather than pushing it to suppliers, and it required a locked design brief. The single sentence in the T5 case literature most relevant to MWAA is that "no UK project had delivered to budget, time and quality" prior to T5 — the base rate was that bad, and T5 beat it by changing the specification and risk-holding model, not by working harder inside the same model. [Source: https://infrastructuredeliverymodels.gihub.org/case-studies/heathrow-terminal-5/]

### 2.3 The MacLeamy arithmetic applied to acoustic/PA/EVACS

Acoustic treatment, PA zoning, FIDS placement, and EVACS intelligibility are the classic examples of requirements that are cheap in schematic design and expensive after construction because they drive:

- Ceiling geometry and ceiling void depth (structural and MEP coordination)
- Wall assemblies and STC ratings (envelope specifications)
- Loudspeaker density, aiming, and tap counts (electrical routing and rough-in)
- FIDS sightlines (millwork, tenant lease-line, and signage coordination)
- Assistive-listening looping and captioning infrastructure (below-slab and above-ceiling rough-in)

ACRP Report 175 puts a concrete number on retrofit: **3,200 m² of suspended baffles plus 800 m² of wall panels at $2.8M**, or approximately $700/m² of treated area, for one representative terminal remediation. That number does not include operational disruption, contractor mobilization at a live terminal, or the cost of replacing a PA head-end that was specified against the wrong intelligibility target. In new construction, the same acoustic performance is achieved by specifying ceiling and wall assemblies that are only marginally more expensive than the base case (and often identical after value engineering), because the labor and general conditions are already priced. [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrp0715/wp-content/themes/acrp-child/documents/201/original/acrp_r175.pdf]

The design literature converges on a ~20× multiplier for late change. Applied to a $22.5B program, a 1% acoustic scope that costs $225M to specify correctly at schematic design costs $4.5B if it enters as change orders. That is the order of magnitude the strategist should hold in mind.

### 2.4 Cost per enplanement, debt service, and financial headroom

IAD's FY2024 CPE was approximately $12.88, near the median for the 47 large-hub US airports (which ranged from $3.93 at ATL to $36.01 at JFK in FY2024). MWAA's Official Statement for the June 2025 issuance forecasts CPE reaching ~$20.71 by 2030 — a trajectory that reflects the pre-expansion $9.0B plan, not the announced $20–22.5B envelope. If the airline signatories are asked to fund the expanded program at rates and charges, IAD CPE could plausibly move to the upper quartile of large-hub CPE. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airport-finance/large-hub/cpe] [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/airports/iad]

Financial capacity to absorb the standard is not the constraint. MWAA carries Aa3/AA-/AA- ratings, historical DSCR of 2.77× (2023) and forecast 2.54× (2024) against a 1.40× U&L target, and ~$4.9B outstanding debt with ~$5.5B of planned issuance through 2028. What is constrained is airline patience with rate increases and the political optics of a rising CPE curve at a hub whose largest signatory (United) has just signed a long-term lease. Every dollar of avoidable rework becomes an issue at the next rates hearing. [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/editorial-section/debt-management]

### 2.5 The opportunity cost argument, priced

At IAD's expected FY2025 enplanements of ~13.9M, a $10M rework cost added to the rate base pumps CPE by ~$0.72 for one year, or spreads across a bond life of 25 years as debt service. That is a small number until it is stacked against the pipeline: AeroTrain extension ($3.75B), Concourse C/D replacement, mobile lounge retirement infrastructure, and the DCA Terminal 1 replacement. Every $100M of avoidable acoustic/PA rework is roughly a **1.3–1.5% increment on the DCA T1 program envelope** (~$2.39B DCA program), or a similar-order increment on any one Dulles concourse package.

The stronger opportunity cost argument is capacity, not dollars. MWAA's engineering, program-management, commissioning, and design-review staff are not scalable on demand; every hour spent retrofitting acoustic and PA performance in Concourse E after opening is an hour not spent on the C/D replacement or the AeroTrain extension. In a program of this size and complexity, the binding constraint is executive attention and commissioning throughput, not capital. [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/05/12/report-new-plan-to-overhaul-dulles-airport-would-eliminate-mobile-lounges/]

