## 3. Caveats and limitations

- **No primary MWAA acoustic data.** The airport context packet explicitly names "executed acoustic performance of existing IAD and DCA terminal spaces" as a gap. I have not been able to source measured ambient level, reverberation time, or STI values for existing MWAA terminal spaces. All economic argument assumes representative rather than measured baseline conditions.
- **Funding structure of the expanded Dulles program is unresolved.** As of the July 2026 announcement, MWAA and DOT have not disclosed how the $20–22.5B envelope will be funded (rates and charges, general airport revenue bonds, federal contribution, private participation). Trade coverage (Bond Buyer, FFXnow) has flagged this as an open question. All CPE trajectories in the current Official Statement predate the expansion.
- **ACRP retrofit cost is a single data point.** The $2.8M / 3,200 m² baffle + 800 m² panel figure in ACRP 175 is representative, not statistically pooled. Real retrofit costs at MWAA scale would need to be sourced from bid history or from a controlled construction estimator.
- **Cost overrun cases are selection-biased.** BER, Denver, LAX APM, and IAD AeroTrain are famous because they failed; the reference class of terminal projects that worked and did not become case studies is larger. Heathrow T5 is included precisely to counteract this selection bias, but the empirical claim "megaprojects run over" is stronger than any point estimate I can defend.
- **MacLeamy 20× multiplier is a design-literature convention, not a measured airport-specific value.** I have used it as an order-of-magnitude anchor, not as a precise coefficient. A quantitative-analyst pass should replace it with an airport-specific rework multiplier if one exists.
- **I have not verified funding-structure or MII terms of the 2025 U&L.** Any claim about airline signatory ability to block a standard on cost grounds is analytical inference, not a reading of the executed agreement.

